# GUI test protocol

How three agents get one agentTools test of Controllers to pass on one desk.
<Test> is the test class; <desk> is the name of the desk in the on("<desk>",...) entries of the tests; <workspace> is the fearlessBranch folder of the coordinator (for example /data/fearlessBranch1).
The protocol is the same for this machine (ubuntu_gnome) and for a VM: only opening and closing differ.
Every test empties the share /data/pilotio and copies the app back into it before it starts, so nothing of the protocol lives there: its files are in /data/pilot_<desk>.

## Roles

- The coordinator: the session that runs the protocol. It starts the other two agents and passes the messages between them; it runs no step of Pre, Post, Self consistency or Finally, and it never answers an action of the test.
- The operator: Opus 5.5, medium effort, started by the coordinator. It runs Pre, Post, Self consistency and Finally.
- The person: Haiku 5.5, low effort, started by the coordinator with the prompt of "The person". It answers the actions of the test.
The coordinator must be able to start agents.
A coordinator that cannot start agents (for example an agent started by another agent) fails loudly: it runs nothing of the protocol, tells the user it cannot start the operator and the person, and stops.

## Coordinator

1. Start the operator with this message, with <Test>, <desk> and <workspace> written out:
   `You are the operator of the GUI test protocol <workspace>/ZeroToHero/tasks/GuiTestProtocol, for <Test> on <desk>, in <workspace>. Run Pre. End your reply with "passed" if Pre 3 passed and you ran Finally, or with "ready" if the test runs in agent mode.`
2. If the operator replied "passed", report its reply to the user: the protocol is over.
3. Start the person with the prompt of "The person", with <Test>, <desk> and <workspace> written out.
4. When the person replies, send its whole reply to the operator: `The person reported:` followed by the reply, then `Run Post.`
5. If the operator replied "restarted", send the person `The test started again.` and go to 4.
6. Otherwise the operator ran Finally: report its reply to the user.

## Pre (operator)

Whenever a run of <Test> fails, the operator first goes through Self consistency.

1. Run the align-branches skill in <workspace>.
2. If <desk> is a VM: `/data/AgentsCoordination/linuxVersion/autoScripts/vm.sh open <desk>`
   If <desk> is ubuntu_gnome: bring the desk back to its fixed shape with the commands of "The desk of this machine" in /data/AgentsCoordination/linuxVersion/installation.txt.
3. Run the recorded values, from <workspace>/Coordinator/Build/src:
   `/opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea --module-path ../../../Commons/Commons.jar --add-modules Commons scripts/TestAgentTools.java <desk> false <Test> /data/pilot_<desk>/channel /data/pilotio`
   If it passes, go to Finally.
   This run is the cheapest way to know whether the recorded values work, so it always runs: when the <desk> entries hold val(null) it fails at the first of them; that is the expected result, and Pre goes on.
4. In <workspace>/Controllers/test/agentTools, set every val(...) of the <desk> entries that <Test> uses (in <Test>.java and ManagerTest.java) to val(null).
5. `mkdir -p /data/pilot_<desk>/channel /data/pilot_<desk>/shots`
6. Start the test in agent mode, detached from the shell of the operator, from <workspace>/Coordinator/Build/src:
   `setsid -f /opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea --module-path ../../../Commons/Commons.jar --add-modules Commons scripts/TestAgentTools.java <desk> true <Test> /data/pilot_<desk>/channel /data/pilotio > /data/pilot_<desk>/run.txt 2>&1`
   Then end the reply with "ready".

## The person

The prompt of the person is below.
The list of mistakes at its end holds what earlier runs did wrong; the operator adds a line when a run shows a new one, and says so in its reply.

```
You are the person at the desk in the agentTools test <Test> (<workspace>/Controllers/test/agentTools/<Test>.java; shared actions in ManagerTest.java), on the desk <desk>.
The screen of this machine shows that desk, ready. The test is already running and waits for you.

At each action the test creates an empty file named after the action in /data/pilot_<desk>/channel and waits.
The on("<desk>",...) entry of that action says what to answer: each val(null) is one value, in order.
Answer by writing the values into that file, one per line, then a line ;
- on("<desk>", () -> waitClick(val(null), val(null), val(null))) for action managerMenu, first the milliseconds to wait after the end of the previous action, then where to click: `printf '100\n412\n88\n;\n' > /data/pilot_<desk>/channel/managerMenu`
- on("<desk>", () -> waitDoubleClick(val(null), val(null), val(null))) for action openData, first the milliseconds to wait after the end of the previous action, then where to double click: `printf '100\n412\n88\n;\n' > /data/pilot_<desk>/channel/openData`
- on("<desk>", () -> waitAfter(val(null))) for action managerShown: `printf '100\n;\n' > /data/pilot_<desk>/channel/managerShown`
- an entry () -> waitKeys(val(null), KeyEvent.VK_ALT, KeyEvent.VK_D): the keys are fixed, only the wait is a value: `printf '100\n;\n' > /data/pilot_<desk>/channel/<action>`
- an entry () -> waitClickKeys(val(null), val(null), val(null), KeyEvent.VK_ALT, KeyEvent.VK_SHIFT, KeyEvent.VK_Q): the wait and where to click, then the test presses the keys
- a text value is quoted: "hello"
Which action waits: `ls /data/pilot_<desk>/channel`
The screen: `/opt/jdk-26.0.2/bin/java -ea -cp <workspace>/out/modular/controller-test:<workspace>/Commons/Commons.jar agentTools.Pilot seen /data/pilot_<desk>/shots/<n>.png`, then read the png; a purple ring marks the pointer.
The test output: /data/pilot_<desk>/run.txt; when the test ends it shows how many tests succeeded and failed.
A wait value is for later runs without you: in this run you are slower than any wait, so every wait is already over when the test reads it, and that is intended.
A wait is 100 unless you expect the step to take longer.
What the manager should do: <workspace>/ZeroToHero/tasks/ControllersDesign. Your role: "Testing GUIs" in CLAUDE.md.

A failure has one of three causes:
(1) a value you gave was wrong, for example the pointer missed a target the screen shows;
(2) the test checks behaviour the design does not describe;
(3) a step of the test is not what a person does on this desk, or needs an adjustment for it.
A step that does nothing on this desk is (3), even if the test passes.
Stop at the first (2) or (3). Do not fix the test.
Change no file except the files of /data/pilot_<desk>/channel and /data/pilot_<desk>/shots.

Mistakes made in earlier runs of this prompt:
- a click answered on a step that did nothing on the desk (a click on an empty corner), called harmless instead of reported as (3);
- a value answered without looking at the screen first.
- an action whose entry is only a waitAfter reported as a step that does nothing: a waitAfter entry is a wait, never a finding.

Report: for each action, the values you gave, what the screen showed, and why (for a wait above 100, why longer); how the test ended; for a failure, its cause 1, 2 or 3 with the evidence, and for (1) the better values.
End the report with one line per action, in the order the test asked them, with the exact values you wrote: `name: v1, v2, v3`.
After a (1), the test starts again and you get the message "The test started again.": answer the new run.
```

## Post (operator)

1. Report (1): stop the test (Finally 1), Pre 6 again, then end the reply with "restarted".
2. Report (2) or (3): `git -C <workspace>/Controllers checkout test/agentTools`, put the finding in the reply, and go to Finally.
3. Passed: write the values of the last run into the <desk> entries, then run Pre 3 three times.
   The values count only when all three runs pass: the shortest wait that passes once can fail the next time.
4. If a run of Pre 3 fails: from the failing check and `agentTools.Pilot seen`, judge which earlier waits may be too short (not every action is followed by a check that verifies it), raise them with informed guesses, and run Pre 3 three times again.
   A wait above 500 gets a comment saying why. A failure that time does not explain goes in the reply, with the test unchanged.
5. A passing <desk> entry set goes into the open Controllers PR of recorded entry sets, if one is open (`gh pr view <n> --repo FearlessLang/Controllers --json state`); otherwise into a new one.

## Self consistency (operator)

The test files are <workspace>/Controllers/test/agentTools/<Test>.java and ManagerTest.java.
Every test builds all the actions of ManagerTest, and building an action looks up its <desk> entry: a ManagerTest action with no <desk> entry makes every test fail on <desk>, also the tests that never do that action.
The checks:
1. Every action of ManagerTest and of <Test> has exactly one entry for each of the 13 desks.
2. In <Test> and ManagerTest, every step of the person comes after a wait: no entry calls a plain click, doubleClick, rightClick, drag, keys or type; the entries call waitClick, waitDoubleClick, waitRightClick, waitDrag, waitKeys, waitType or waitClickKeys.
3. Every wait above 500 has a comment saying why.
A wait above 500 in the <desk> entries of <Test> gets its comment in Post 4.
Any other failing check is a finding: put it in the reply with the check, and go on with the protocol.

## Finally (operator, always)

The operator always ends with Finally, whatever happened before, and then replies with the outcome: passed with the PR of the values, or the finding.


1. Stop the test, if it still runs (in agent mode it waits for its channel file forever):
   `ps -C java -o pid=,args= | awk '/TestAgentTools.java <desk> |-Ddesk=<desk> /{print $1}' | xargs -r kill`
2. If <desk> is a VM: `/data/AgentsCoordination/linuxVersion/autoScripts/vm.sh close <desk>`
3. `rm -rf /data/pilot_<desk>`
4. Check the desk is bare: `ps -eo pid,comm | awk '$2 ~ /^(fearlessManaged|nautilus|gnome-terminal-|ptyxis|gnome-text-edit)/'` prints nothing.
